{
  "title": "vLLM's qwen_3_coder structural tag rejects zero-argument calls in Qwen3's format; with an any_text body it swallows the next call",
  "description": "UPSTREAM, in vllm/tool_parsers/structural_tag_registry.py (0.27.1, also 0.26.0). The builder's begin ends with a newline and its end = '\\n</function>\\n</tool_call>' starts with one, so a zero-argument call needs TWO newlines between <function=NAME> and </function>; Qwen3 writes ONE. In vLLM's own schema-bodied tag that REJECTS a correct zero-argument call. In our tool_name_guard, which deliberately uses an any_text body to constrain only the name, the tag instead cannot close after a zero-argument call and swallows the next call unconstrained -- the schema eval's g14 (a valid get_action_choices, then a URL as a tool name) was accepted by the exact tag and killed the pilot. FIXED LOCALLY on this branch: tool_name_guard.END = '</function>\\n</tool_call>', with vLLM's value kept as VLLM_END so the deviation is named; pipelines/opd/check_guard_syntax.py (mtg repo, s/check-guard-end-deviation) pins it and replays g14 as its control. Validated over ~28,700 real completions from both eval arms: 97 verdicts change, every one a correct rejection -- 94 bound choose_action choices OUTSIDE their enum that the swallowing had let through, 2 invented tool names, 1 hallucinated argument the engine ignored -- and no genuine completion is newly rejected. So the bug also BYPASSED the bound choice on the second call of any response whose first call took no arguments. The upstream report below is ready to post; every code block in it was executed verbatim and prints exactly the values its comments claim. NOT POSTED: whether to file it upstream, and under whose account, is Eugenio's call.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 2,
  "type": "bug",
  "labels": [
    "upstream",
    "vllm",
    "grammar",
    "tool-name-guard",
    "decision-schema"
  ],
  "created_at": "2026-09-22T05:00:00.000-03:00",
  "updated_at": "2026-09-22T05:00:00.000-03:00",
  "upstream_report_markdown": "### qwen_3_coder structural tag rejects zero-argument tool calls in Qwen3's own format\n\n**vLLM 0.27.1** (also present in 0.26.0), `vllm/tool_parsers/structural_tag_registry.py`, `qwen_3_coder` builder.\n\nFor each tool the builder emits\n`begin = \"<tool_call>\\n<function={name}>\\n\"` and `end = \"\\n</function>\\n</tool_call>\"`.\n`begin` ends with a newline and `end` starts with one, so for a tool call with **no parameters**\nthe grammar requires **two** newlines between `<function=NAME>` and `</function>`. Qwen3 emits\n**one**: `<tool_call>\\n<function=NAME>\\n</function>\\n</tool_call>`. Under grammar-constrained\ndecoding, a correct zero-argument call in the model's own format is therefore rejected, and the\nmodel is pushed onto an off-distribution token.\n\n**Reproduction** (vLLM + xgrammar only; no model or GPU needed):\n\n```python\nimport json\nimport xgrammar as xgr\nfrom xgrammar.testing import _is_grammar_accept_string as accepts\nfrom vllm.entrypoints.openai.chat_completion import protocol\nfrom vllm.tool_parsers import structural_tag_registry as registry\n\nno_args = {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {}, \"required\": [], \"additionalProperties\": False}\ntools = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"get_state\", \"strict\": True, \"parameters\": no_args}}]\nreq = protocol.ChatCompletionRequest(model=\"m\", messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"x\"}],\n                                     tools=tools, tool_choice=\"auto\")\ntag = registry.get_model_structural_tag(model=\"qwen_3_coder\", tools=req.tools,\n                                        tool_choice=\"auto\", reasoning=False).model_dump()\ng = xgr.Grammar.from_structural_tag(json.dumps(tag))\n\nprint(accepts(g, \"<tool_call>\\n<function=get_state>\\n</function>\\n</tool_call>\"))    # False -- Qwen3's format\nprint(accepts(g, \"<tool_call>\\n<function=get_state>\\n\\n</function>\\n</tool_call>\"))  # True\n```\n\n**Worse with a free-text body.** A caller that constrains only the tool *name* (tag content\n`{\"type\": \"any_text\"}`, same `begin`/`end`) gets a tag that cannot close after a zero-argument\ncall — its body keeps consuming until the next `\\n</function>\\n</tool_call>`, which belongs to the\n**following** call. That call is then unconstrained, so a name that was never offered passes:\n\n```python\nfor t in tag[\"format\"][\"tags\"]:\n    t[\"content\"] = {\"type\": \"any_text\"}\ng2 = xgr.Grammar.from_structural_tag(json.dumps(tag))\none = \"<tool_call>\\n<function=get_state>\\n</function>\\n</tool_call>\"\nbad = \"<tool_call>\\n<function=not_a_tool>\\n</function>\\n</tool_call>\"\nprint(accepts(g2, bad))              # False -- the name constraint works on a first call\nprint(accepts(g2, one + \"\\n\" + bad)) # True  -- but not after a zero-argument call\n```\n\nThe tool-call parser splits on delimiters and still extracts both calls. We hit this in production:\nthe model emitted a valid zero-argument call followed by a hallucinated tool name, the grammar\naccepted it, and the harness received a call to a tool that did not exist.\n\n**Proposed fix:** `end = \"</function>\\n</tool_call>\"` (drop the leading newline). The newline\nbefore `</function>` is then either the one `begin` consumed (zero arguments) or part of the\nparameter block (`...</parameter>\\n`). With that change both reproductions behave, and the schema\nbody still accepts real calls with parameters:\n\n```python\nschema_content = registry.get_model_structural_tag(          # a fresh, unmodified vLLM tag\n    model=\"qwen_3_coder\", tools=req.tools, tool_choice=\"auto\", reasoning=False\n).model_dump()\nfor tg in (schema_content, tag):                             # schema body, and the any_text body\n    for t in tg[\"format\"][\"tags\"]:\n        t[\"end\"] = \"</function>\\n</tool_call>\"\nfixed_schema = xgr.Grammar.from_structural_tag(json.dumps(schema_content))\nfixed_text = xgr.Grammar.from_structural_tag(json.dumps(tag))\nprint(accepts(fixed_schema, one))            # True  -- Qwen3's zero-arg format is accepted\nprint(accepts(fixed_text, one + \"\\n\" + bad)) # False -- the unoffered name no longer passes\nprint(accepts(fixed_text, bad))              # False -- first-call behaviour unchanged\n```\n\nWe validated the change against ~28,700 real completions from a Qwen3.5-4B fine-tune served by\n0.27.1: it newly rejects 97, every one of which contains a call the grammar should never have\nallowed (94 of them a parameter value outside its enum, which the swallowed second call had\nescaped), and it newly rejects no correct completion.\n"
}
